#import "template.typ": *

#heading(numbering: none)[Glossary]

Terms are listed alphabetically. The chapter where each term first appears is in brackets.

#term[Air burst][A burst high enough that the fireball never touches the surface below. [2]]
#term[Absolute pressure][Total pressure counted from a perfect vacuum. Can't be negative. [0]]
#term[Acoustic impedance ($rho c$)][Density times sound speed; how "stiff" a material is to pressure waves. About $1.5 times 10^6$ kg/(m² s) for seawater. [1]]
#term[Ambient pressure][The normal pressure at a location before the explosion: air pressure plus the weight of the water above. [0]]
#term[Arrival time ($t_a$)][When the shock front reaches a receiver: distance divided by sound speed. [1]]
#term[Bubble][The ball of hot gas and steam created by an underwater explosion. It expands and contracts several times. [1]]
#term[Cavitation][Water tearing apart into vapour bubbles when pulled too hard (pressure near or below zero). [0, 1]]
#term[Critical angle][The steepest angle (about 13° from vertical) at which sound from air can still enter water as a travelling wave. [2]]
#term[Contact burst][A burst within a few metres of the surface (5 W#super[0.3] ft). [2]]
#term[Cube-root scaling][An explosion $k$ times bigger produces the same effect $k^(1\/3)$ times farther away. [0, 1]]
#term[Energy flux density ($E$)][Shock energy passing through each square metre, in J/m². [1]]
#term[Evanescent zone][Where the blast front sweeps across the water slower than sound travels in water; pressure fades with depth. [2]]
#term[Exponential decay][Shrinking by the same fraction in each equal time step. [0]]
#term[Fireball][The glowing ball of extremely hot gas formed by a nuclear explosion. [2]]
#term[Friedlander waveform][The standard blast-pulse shape $P (1 - t/D) e^(-b t\/D)$. [2]]
#term[Free field][Conditions far from any surface or sea floor, so only the direct wave matters. [1]]
#term[Height of burst (HOB)][How high above the surface the weapon explodes. [2]]
#term[Image source][An imaginary mirror-image burst used to calculate reflections from the water surface. [1]]
#term[Impulse ($I$)][Area under the pressure--time curve: "how hard, for how long". Units Pa·s or psi·s. [1]]
#term[Isovelocity water][Water in which the speed of sound is the same everywhere. [1]]
#term[Kiloton (kt)][Energy of 1,000 tonnes of TNT, defined as $4.184 times 10^12$ J. A megaton (Mt) is 1,000 kt. [0]]
#term[Mach stem][The single, stronger front formed where a blast wave and its reflection off the surface merge. [2]]
#term[Model][A simplified mathematical description of reality, valid within known limits. [0]]
#term[Negative phase][The weaker, longer suction that follows a blast wave's positive push. Not modelled yet. [2]]
#term[Nuclear shock factor ($f$)][Fraction of nuclear yield that acts like TNT for the water shock, about 0.49 in this simulator. [1]]
#term[Overpressure][Pressure above ambient. A shock is described by its overpressure. [0]]
#term[Positive-phase duration ($D$)][How long the overpressure stays above zero. [2]]
#term[Propagating zone][Near ground zero, where the blast front sweeps across the water faster than sound in water and the pulse travels straight down. [2]]
#term[Pascal (Pa)][SI unit of pressure: one newton per square metre. 1 atm ≈ 101,325 Pa; 1 psi ≈ 6,895 Pa. [0]]
#term[Peak overpressure ($P_m$)][The highest overpressure reached when the shock front arrives. [1]]
#term[Power law][A relationship $y = C x^a$. A straight line on log--log axes. [0]]
#term[psi][Pounds (force) per square inch, a US pressure unit. [0]]
#term[Receiver][Anything placed in the simulation to "feel" the effects: a fish, a person, a gauge, a ship. [0]]
#term[Scaled range ($lambda$)][Distance divided by the cube root of charge weight, $R \/ W^(1\/3)$. [1]]
#term[Shock wave][A thin front across which pressure jumps almost instantly, travelling at or above the speed of sound. [1]]
#term[Similitude equations][Power-law fits to measured explosion data, written in terms of scaled range. [1]]
#term[Slant range][Straight-line distance from the burst to a point, including the depth difference. [0]]
#term[Surface cutoff][The sudden end of the shock pulse near the surface, caused by the reflected tension wave. [1]]
#term[Time constant ($theta$)][Time for an exponentially decaying pressure to fall to 37% ($1 \/ e$) of its peak. [0, 1]]
#term[Trace speed ($V$)][The speed at which the blast front sweeps across the water surface. [2]]
#term[Yield][Total energy released by a nuclear weapon, in kilotons or megatons of TNT equivalent. [0]]
